  11:  * at all: the transient `jwt_expiry` change the live proof needs is made and reverted inside that
  12:  * script, and the final `git diff` proves the file unchanged.
  13:  *
  14:  * WHAT THE FIXTURE REACHES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT, said before the first assertion because it is the
  15:  * one thing a reader could get wrong here. The confirmations flip is a LIVE-STACK behaviour: the
  16:  * real GoTrue issues no session at signup and refuses sign-in until the address is confirmed. The
  17:  * fixture's storage is a Map and its `Session` is a handle, not an access token â€” nothing here is
  18:  * signed, decoded or parsed. What the fixture DOES now model is the session's LIFETIME: an
  19:  * `auth.sessions` row per session, an expiry one hour out mirroring `jwt_expiry = 3600`, and
  20:  * revocation. Which sessions are live is that mirror; whether a dead session yields a caller is the
  21:  * SHIPPED `callerFromAuthAnswer`, the same judgement both deployed edge functions run. The live
  22:  * behaviour is measured by hand, in `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/proof-local.ts` checks (a), (c) and (d)
  23:  * and in `loop/items/AI4DEV-60/proof-local.ts` checks (a) to (e) and (g).
  24:  *
  25:  * THE ORDER THE FOUR NEW BODIES FOLLOW IS THE LIVE PUBLIC ONE: register, use the emailed
  26:  * verification link, SIGN IN, and only then complete signup or play the session and password games.
  27:  * That order is deliberate and narrows the one divergence this suite carries â€” the fixture mints a
  28:  * session at registration and the live stack with confirmations on mints none, which `_contract.ts`
  29:  * writes out on `registerWithEmailPassword`. The registration handle plays no part in any assertion
  30:  * the four bodies make.
  31:  *
  32:  * WHICH LEAVES ONE HONEST GAP, and it is stated rather than hidden: at THIS tier a
  33:  * completed-but-unverified account is constructible, and AT-001.10's body constructs one on
  34:  * purpose. On the live stack the public path cannot reach that state. The gate is still the right
  35:  * thing to ship and to test, because decision-8 makes verification the WRITE PATH's own floor â€”
  36:  * "the floor for any Discovery message" â€” and not a property borrowed from the session layer.
  37:  * Non-public paths and configuration history both produce exactly this state, and a floor that
  38:  * only holds while the session layer holds is not a floor.
  39:  *
  40:  * NO LINK-LIFETIME SEMANTICS ARE ASSERTED ANYWHERE BELOW â€” not for a verification link and not for
  41:  * a reset link. AT-001.11 is retired ("verification-link expiry/single-use/resend semantics are not
  42:  * stated in REQ-001", `.taskmaster/docs/acceptance/at-req-001.md` line 22) and AT-001.15 is retired
  43:  * for the reset link on line 30. Both bodies that follow a never-issued link do so to guard their
  44:  * OWN ORACLE â€” a link-shaped string that worked would make "the emailed link is what does it" mean
  45:  * nothing â€” and neither makes any claim about how long a real link lasts or how often it may be
  46:  * used. AT-001.14's own note also drops the audit claim, so no audit row is written or read here.
  47:  *
  48:  * SURFACE MARKS, and this leaf's are the more interesting half.
  49:  *   - AT-001.09 (`ui`) and AT-001.14 (`ui`) are two of the four auth screens the manifest's wiring
  50:  *     leaf names â€” "signup, sign-in, verification, reset" â€” so that leaf must find them and re-run
  51:  *     them wired. AT-001.38 (`ui`) is the sign-in screen's negative path, the third of the four.
  52:  *   - AT-001.10 stays `backend`: the Discovery composer is REQ-002/004's surface, not one of those
  53:  *     four screens, and the wiring leaf has nothing of it to re-run. AT-001.12 is `backend` for the
  54:  *     same kind of reason â€” expiry and revocation are the session layer's enforcement and no named
  55:  *     screen carries them.
  56:  *   - AT-001.13 IS `ui`, AND THE REASON IS THE MARK'S MECHANISM RATHER THAN A SCREEN. Its criterion
  57:  *     says the session refreshes AUTOMATICALLY, and the thing that schedules a refresh is the
  58:  *     supabase-js client. No client exists in this tree, so this body proves the mechanism and not
  59:  *     the scheduling. A `backend` mark would orphan the automatic clause â€” the wiring leaf finds
  60:  *     its work by the `ui` mark, so nothing before the requirement's far-away integration-tier gate
  61:  *     would ever exercise it. The `ui` mark is the handoff: the wired re-run drives this id through
  62:  *     the real client, where automatic refresh is the thing under test.
  63:  */
  64: 
  65: import { expect } from 'vitest';
  66: import { atTest } from './_bind.ts';
  67: // The INTEGRATION-tier procedures for the ids whose criteria are proved differently against a real
  68: // stack. Same criterion, same id, one registration; only the procedure differs. See _integration.ts.
  69: import { at00109, at00110, at00112, at00113, at00114, at00138 } from './_integration.ts';
  70: import type { AccountsSut } from './_contract.ts';
  71: // THE TWO EMAIL-CAPABLE PUBLIC TYPES, read from the shipped vocabulary rather than spelled as a
  72: // pair of literals. AT-001.09's own words are "EITHER account type that can register by email (NGO
  73: // and volunteer)", and `PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES` is that set as a value: the platform
  74: // administrator is provisioned rather than signed up, so `parseAccountType` refuses it and it is
  75: // outside this criterion by construction. Iterating the constant makes "either account type" a
  76: // fact of the run instead of a claim in a test name.
  77: import { PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES } from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/accounts.ts';
  78: 
  79: /** The version string of the ToS + Platform Promise text these tests accept on the user's behalf. */
  80: const TEXT_VERSION = 'tos-2026-01+promise-2026-01';
  81: /** The address the acknowledgment records â€” the same reported-not-verified value section A uses. */
  82: const CLIENT_IP = '203.0.113.7';
  83: /** The password every email/password registration in this file uses. */
  84: const PASSWORD = 'correct horse battery staple';
  85: 
  86: atTest(
  87:   'AT-001.09',
  88:   'a fresh email/password signup of either account type is unverified until the link is used',
  89:   { surface: 'ui' },
  90:   {
  91:     default: async ({ open }) => {
  92:       const { w, sut } = await open();
  93:   
  94:       // THE ORDERING IS DELIBERATE AND IS STATED SO IT CAN BE ATTACKED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED. This
  95:       // body completes signup BEFORE verifying, so that the TYPED account is observably unverified â€”
  96:       // the criterion says "the account ... is email-unverified until the emailed verification link
  97:       // is used", and "account" read as the typed account is what makes clause 2 below meaningful.
  98:       // On the live stack with confirmations on, the public order is the other one: verify, then
  99:       // sign in, then complete. The live proof drives THAT order against the real stack, check (e).
 100:       // The two orders together cover both readings of "account", and neither contradicts any
 101:       // criterion.
 102:       for (const accountType of PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ACCOUNT_TYPES) {
 103:         const email = w.email(`verify-${accountType}`);
 104:   
 105:         // (1) A FRESH SIGNUP IS UNVERIFIED. This is the half that fails against an implementation
 106:         // which confirms addresses at registration, and it has to be read BEFORE anything else
 107:         // happens to the account.
 108:         const session = await sut.registerWithEmailPassword(email, PASSWORD);
 109:         expect(
 110:           await sut.emailVerified(session.accountId),
 111:           `a fresh ${accountType} email/password signup must be email-unverified`,
 112:         ).toBe(false);
 113:   
 114:         // The volunteer branch needs a linked GitHub identity before completion â€” that is AT-001.04's
 115:         // requirement, not this one's, and it is satisfied here rather than tested.
 116:         if (accountType === 'volunteer') {
 117:           await sut.linkGithubIdentity(session, 'riverside-verifier');
 118:         }
 119:   
 120:         // (2) THE ACCOUNT IS TYPED, and the row says which type. Without this the loop would prove
 121:         // the same thing twice about two indistinguishable accounts, and "either account type" would
 122:         // be a claim in the test's name rather than a fact of the run.
 123:         const completion = await sut.completeSignup(
 124:           session,
 125:           {
 126:             accountType,
 127:             organizationName: accountType === 'ngo' ? 'Riverside Shelter' : undefined,
 128:             acknowledgmentTextVersion: TEXT_VERSION,
 129:           },
 130:           CLIENT_IP,
 131:         );
 132:         expect(completion, `the ${accountType} completion was refused`).toMatchObject({ ok: true });
 133:         if (!completion.ok) return;
 134:         expect(await sut.account(completion.accountId)).toEqual({
 135:           id: session.accountId,
 136:           accountType,
 137:         });
 138:   
 139:         // (3) COMPLETING SIGNUP DOES NOT VERIFY THE ADDRESS. An implementation that flipped the flag
 140:         // as a side effect of completion would satisfy the final assertion of this test while making
 141:         // the emailed link decorative.
 142:         expect(
 143:           await sut.emailVerified(session.accountId),
 144:           `completing ${accountType} signup must not verify the email address`,
 145:         ).toBe(false);
 146:   
 147:         // (4) A LINK THAT WAS NEVER ISSUED FLIPS NOTHING. This is not a claim about link lifetime â€”
 148:         // retired AT-001.11's ground, deliberately untouched. It guards THIS TEST'S OWN ORACLE: if a
 149:         // link-shaped string verified an account it never belonged to, then "using the emailed link
 150:         // is what flips it" would mean nothing, because any string would flip anything.
 151:         const neverIssued = await sut.useVerificationLink('never-issued-link');
 152:         expect(neverIssued.ok, 'a verification link that was never issued must not succeed').toBe(false);
 153:         expect(
 154:           await sut.emailVerified(session.accountId),
 155:           'a verification link that was never issued flipped the account to verified',
 156:         ).toBe(false);
 157:   
 158:         // (5) USING THE EMAILED LINK IS WHAT FLIPS IT â€” the criterion's second clause, and the
 159:         // discriminating change. Everything else about the account is identical across this line.
 160:         const link = await sut.emailedVerificationLink(email);
 161:         expect(link, `no verification link was emailed to the ${accountType} address`).not.toBeNull();
 162:         const used = await sut.useVerificationLink(link!);
 163:         expect(used.ok, 'the emailed verification link was refused').toBe(true);
 164:         expect(
 165:           await sut.emailVerified(session.accountId),
 166:           'using the emailed verification link did not flip the account to verified',
 167:         ).toBe(true);
 168:   
 169:         // (6) VERIFICATION RE-TYPES NOTHING. The account is the same account, of the same type, and
 170:         // the only thing that changed is the one thing the criterion is about.
 171:         expect(await sut.account(completion.accountId)).toEqual({
 172:           id: session.accountId,
 173:           accountType,
 174:         });
 175:       }
 176:     },
 177:     integration: at00109,
 178:   },
 179: );
